-# 15. Reset, cold boot and warm boot
-
-<!-- Checked against TEC-OS 78127ec, with the starter card's `TED.COM` from
-     Thorium 128edb7 and `TAS.COM` from Thorium dbee66a; the timings below were
-     measured on the host model with that card and the serial board. -->
-
-TEC-OS has two ways to a prompt. Cold boot runs after a reset or a call of
-the BIOS's first entry. It builds everything from nothing: page zero, the
-workspace, the card's drives and the cache. Warm boot runs at the end of
-every program and puts back only what a program may have changed. This
-chapter follows cold boot from the first instruction to the point where it
-runs on into warm boot, then warm boot as far as the CCP. It ends with the
-cost of each in T-states. Chapter 14 maps what each one writes.
-
-## The first instruction
-
-The Z80 starts at `0000h` after a reset and TEC-OS's ROM is at `C000h`. The
-board therefore has to put ROM code at `0000h` for the first instruction. The
-TEC-1G comes out of reset with its system latch at `00h`. The board shows
-the ROM's first 2 KiB, the boot overlay, at `0000h–07FFh` as well as at
-`C000h–C7FFh` while bit 0 of the latch is low. The ROM's first instruction is
-`JP RSTENT` (`src/reset.asm:1-6`), and the Z80 runs it from the overlay at
-`0000h`. Its target is in `C000h–FFFFh`, so the next instruction comes from
-the ROM proper.
-
-`RSTENT` reads the byte at `0002h` to tell a reset from the other way the
-ROM's first bytes are reached (`src/boot.asm:11-22`). With the overlay on,
-that byte is the jump's own high byte, `HIGH(RSTENT)`, and `RSTENT` goes on
-to `COLD`. A program that ends with EXPAND 1 in view, by a return or a jump
-to `0000h`, reaches the same jump another way. The jump at `0000h` leads to
-`BF03h`, which in EXPAND 1 is free space that cold boot cleared. The Z80
-runs the zeros there as `NOP`s on to `C000h`. The overlay is off by then, so
-`0002h` holds `BFh`, the RAM BIOS table's high byte, and `RSTENT` goes to
-warm boot instead. Warm boot puts EXPAND 0 back and writes the cache's dirty
-lines, as it does at any program's end.
-
-`COLD` executes `DI`, loads the stack pointer and writes `LATCLAS`, `01h`, to
-the latch (`src/boot.asm:23-26`). Bit 0 high turns the overlay off, so
-`0000h–07FFh` is RAM again, and the latch now has EXPAND 0 in view
-(`src/layout.asm:17`, `src/layout.asm:21`). Interrupts stay off from here
-unless a program or a service enables them. The ROM's own `EI`s only put back
-a state a routine saved at its entry, as the BIOS's `CEI` does
-(`src/bios.asm:294-300`). The only other `EI` is the one each boot writes at
-`0038h` for a program's `RST 38h` (`src/boot.asm:274-283`). Chapter 18
-describes what interrupts cost a program that enables them.
-
-## Cold boot's steps
-
-`COLD` is one routine, `src/boot.asm:23-83`, and the steps of a cold boot are
-its lines in order:
-
-1. `DI`, the stack at `8000h`, the latch at `LATCLAS` and interrupt mode 1
-   (`src/boot.asm:23-27`).
-2. `RSLCAP`, which reads what the RAM holds from before the reset, if it
-   holds anything, and keeps the drive selection. It runs before anything it
-   reads is cleared (`src/boot.asm:28`).
-3. Page zero cleared, all 256 bytes, and `LATCHV` set to `LATCLAS`
-   (`src/boot.asm:29-35`).
-4. `RAMCHK`, which returns only if there is RAM at `8000h–BFFFh` and EXPAND 1
-   behind it (`src/boot.asm:36`).
-5. The resident workspace cleared, from `WSBASE` to `WSEND`, and the stack
-   moved to the BDOS's stack inside it, which nothing else uses before the CCP
-   runs (`src/boot.asm:37-42`).
-6. `CONINIT`, which starts the serial terminal, reads the Matrix switch and
-   probes for the PICO9918. On a board where the PICO9918 answers and the
-   switch is on, `PISTART` starts the ROM's PICO9918 console at 80 columns.
-   It puts `CRT:` in the IOBYTE, so the banner goes to the screen
-   (`src/console.asm:48-51`, `src/video.asm:8-16`, `src/pico.asm:27-47`).
-   `RTCHAS` then probes for the clock, and the banner is printed
-   (`src/boot.asm:46-53`).
-7. `MEMINIT`, which clears EXPAND 1 and marks every cache line empty
-   (`src/boot.asm:54`).
-8. At `COLDRT`, the BDOS's stack set again, then `LCDINIT`, which clears the
-   20 × 4 LCD, and `TEC-OS 0.1  TEC-1G` on its top row (`src/boot.asm:63-66`,
-   `src/text.asm:53`).
-9. `SDINIT`, which starts the card, and `MOUNT`, which reads the container's
-   table through a cache line, checks it and fills the drive table. Either
-   failure goes to the diagnostic prompt, `DIAG` (`src/boot.asm:67-70`).
-10. `ALVINIT`, which places the allocation vectors and the BDOS entry jump,
-    and `RESEL`, which applies the kept selection if the card still has it
-    (`src/boot.asm:71-72`).
-11. `PZTOP`, the top of the TPA, set to the BDOS entry jump, since no service
-    is loaded yet (`src/boot.asm:73-74`).
-12. The BIOS jump table copied from the ROM to `BF00h` (`src/boot.asm:75-78`).
-13. The volumes printed on the console, a line a drive, and the letters and
-    labels of drives A and B on the LCD's rows 1 and 2 (`src/boot.asm:79-80`,
-    `src/boot.asm:363-383`, `src/lcd.asm:69-89`).
-14. Drive A and user 0 at `0004h` (`src/boot.asm:81-83`). Cold boot then runs
-    on into warm boot, the next routine in the file, whose `PZINIT` builds
-    page zero, once (`src/boot.asm:92-100`).
-
-The order follows from what each step needs. The reset test comes first
-because the steps after it clear the memory it reads. The workspace is cleared
-only after the RAM check, since until then it may not be RAM. Only then does
-the stack move into it. `MEMINIT` comes before the card is started
-because the mount reads the container's table through a cache line, so the
-mount's lines start empty. Step 12 runs at cold boot only, for the reason
-Chapter 16 gives. Chapter 17 describes steps 9 and 10. Chapter 14 describes
-the memory that steps 3, 5 and 10 lay out. The rest of this chapter describes
-steps 2, 4 and 7.
-
-The LCD costs a board without one almost nothing. Before each command or
-character `LCDWAIT` reads the display's busy flag at most 256 times, so a
-missing display costs only that wait (`src/lcd.asm:18-27`). Drives C and D
-have no row. Row 3 is for one of two words TEC-OS shows for a board with no
-terminal. `CARD?` appears at the diagnostic prompt (`src/boot.asm:472-473`),
-and `GLCD?` when `CON UC1` fails to load `GLCD.SVC`, as Chapter 25 describes.
-Appendix G gives the display's ports and commands.
-
-The diagnostic prompt's `R` jumps back to `COLDRT`, step 8
-(`src/boot.asm:487-488`, `src/boot.asm:56-64`). A retry therefore resets the
-stack, clears the LCD, starts the card again and mounts it, with the memory
-uncleared and no second banner. The mount reads every block of the table from
-the card again (`src/vol.asm:32-36`). Chapter 26 describes the prompt.
-
-## The RAM check
-
-TEC-OS needs RAM at `8000h–BFFFh`, because its workspace and stacks are there.
-It needs both halves of U9, because EXPAND 1 holds the cache.
-
-The check, `RAMCHK`, runs before anything has been printed, on the boot stack
-in fixed RAM (`src/boot.asm:314-345`). It writes `55h` at `8000h` and `AAh` at
-`BFFFh`, reads both back and does it again with the patterns swapped. Both
-ends are written before either is read because a board with nothing in U9 has
-a floating data bus. This order stops the bus returning the byte just written
-(`src/boot.asm:314-322`). It then calls `DETECT` to test for EXPAND 1
-at `8000h` with `LATEXP` (`src/boot.asm:327-332`). With either byte wrong or
-the count not 2, `RAMBAD` sends `TEC-OS needs 64K RAM` to the serial port and
-stops in a loop (`src/boot.asm:346-358`). It calls the transmit routine
-`TXBYTE` directly, because the console isn't started yet. That routine uses
-only the port and the stack (`src/console.asm:259-285`).
-
-`DETECT` tests whether setting a given latch bit puts different RAM at a given
-address (`src/boot.asm:190-219`). A bit that selects nothing leaves the same
-RAM in view, so a write made with the bit set still shows once the bit is
-clear. The routine reads the byte at the address with the bit clear, sets the
-bit and reads the byte again. It then writes the complement of the first byte
-and clears the bit. A byte that now differs from the first means the write
-landed in the same RAM: the bit selects nothing and there is one region. A
-byte that is unchanged means the write went to other RAM and there are two.
-Each byte is put back as it was, and the routine returns 1 or 2. Nothing is
-pushed while the bit is set, because the stack itself may be in the RAM the
-bit replaces.
-
-`MEMINIT`, step 7 of cold boot, puts EXPAND 1 in view and clears all 16 KiB
-of it. It fills the cache's 120 bytes of line addresses with `FFh`, which
-marks every line empty, and puts EXPAND 0 back (`src/boot.asm:169-188`).
-
-## The reset test
-
-The TEC-1G's reset button and its power-on reset the Z80 in the same way. The
-difference is in the RAM: after power-on it holds whatever the chips came up
-with, and after the button it still holds what TEC-OS left there. TEC-OS uses
-that to keep one thing across a reset, the selection of volumes on the drives.
-A board whose drives were changed with `MOUNT` comes back from a reset with
-the same drives.
-
-The test is `RSLCAP` (`src/boot.asm:113-167`). The RAM may hold anything at
-this point, so `RSLCAP` runs none of the code it reads. It reads only page
-zero, the word `STUBAD`, the three bytes at the address that word holds and
-the drive table. The RAM passes when all of these hold:
-
-- `0000h` holds a `JP` to `BF03h`, the RAM BIOS table's warm boot entry.
-- `0005h` holds a `JP`.
-- The word in `STUBAD` is the address where cold boot put the BDOS entry
-  jump, and the three bytes there are `JP BENTRY`, the BDOS entry of this ROM.
-- The word at `0006h` is at least `8000h`, and it is that address or a page's
-  start no higher than `STUBAD`'s page.
-
-`RSLCAP` tests the floor of `8000h` before it compares the word with the one
-in `STUBAD` (`src/boot.asm:146-159`). The last condition allows for services,
-which lower the word at `0006h`. The word is not compared with `PZTOP`, so a
-reset pressed to recover from a service's `INIT` that hung still passes
-(`src/boot.asm:113-123`). The test depends on two addresses of this build,
-`STUBAD`'s own and `BENTRY`, so RAM left by a build with either one elsewhere
-fails it.
-
-A reset whose RAM passes the test is a recognised reset, and any other reset
-gives the card's own drives. Cold boot clears page zero at its start and
-writes its jumps again only in warm boot's `PZINIT`, after step 14. A reset
-pressed during cold boot before then, or at the diagnostic prompt, therefore
-fails the test too.
-
-Cold boot is about to clear the drive table. When the RAM passes, `RSLCAP`
-therefore copies the table, 32 bytes, to `RSLBUF` at `0100h` in the TPA
-(`src/boot.asm:160-164`, `src/layout.asm:80-86`). Nothing writes there
-between `COLD` and `RESEL`, so the copy survives the clearing of the workspace
-and is still there for the diagnostic prompt's retry. When the RAM fails,
-`RSLCAP` puts 0 in drive A's geometry byte in `RSLBUF`, which means no
-selection (`src/boot.asm:165-167`).
-
-`RESEL` applies the selection after `MOUNT` has accepted the card in the socket
-and `ALVINIT` has given its drives their vectors in the usual way. Neither the
-slots nor the TPA therefore varies with the selection (`src/vol.asm:468-559`).
-The card in the socket may no longer hold the kept volumes, so `RESEL` works
-in two steps. The first step changes no drive. It walks the card's
-descriptors and compares each with every kept drive in `RSLBUF`. The
-comparison is on the eight bytes a drive-table entry holds: the start, the
-length, the geometry and the flags. A descriptor goes to the first kept drive
-it matches, and the volume must fit that drive's vector slot. The card's own
-drives stand in three cases: a read fails, a matched volume is too big for
-its slot, or a kept drive is left unmatched.
-
-The second step runs only when every kept drive has matched. It copies
-`RSLBUF` over the drive table and the drives' indexes and builds each drive's
-DPH. That step reads nothing and can't fail. A drive that was empty before the
-reset stays empty, even if the card names a volume for it
-(`src/vol.asm:468-482`).
-
-The run below unmounts drive B with `MOUNT B:`, then presses reset. The kept
-selection has no volume on B and the starter card's two volumes still match,
-so after the reset only A is listed:
+# 15. Reset and boot
+
+<!-- Source mechanisms read at adopted TEC-OS
+     962853f499f05faf281f6cdff1deca8959f58c2b. All unprefixed source ranges
+     below use that revision. This is source inspection, not artifact qualification. -->
+
+<!-- Historical evidence: both retained captures and all measured timings
+     belong to TEC-OS 78127ec, with the starter card's TED.COM from Thorium
+     128edb7 and TAS.COM from Thorium dbee66a. The timings used the host model
+     and serial board. Original evidence: book base10821b6,
+     tec-os/15-reset-and-boot.md. No capture or measurement was rerun. -->
+
+A program can finish and leave the next command prompt ready without resetting
+the board. Warm boot rebuilds the program environment while retaining the drive
+selection, services and history. Cold boot follows a reset or a call to the
+BIOS's first entry. It checks RAM and reconstructs the resident workspace, card
+mount and block cache before entering the same warm-boot path
+(`src/boot.asm:49-158`, `src/boot.asm:178-215`). The clearing spans and retained
+regions are mapped in
+[Chapter 14](14-state-and-memory.md#the-workspace-across-a-program-exit).
+
+## The reset instruction
+
+The Z80 starts at `0000h` after reset but TEC-OS resides at `C000h`.
+The TEC-1G's latch resets to `00h`, which makes the first 2 KiB of ROM
+visible at `0000h–07FFh` as well as `C000h–C7FFh`. Latch bit 0 controls
+this boot overlay. The first instruction is `JP RSTENT`, whose target is
+in high ROM. After that jump the CPU can remove the overlay without
+removing the code it is executing (`src/reset.asm:1-14`,
+`src/layout.asm:26-45`, `src/boot.asm:49-56`).
+
+`RSTENT` reads `0002h`. At reset the overlay supplies the high byte of
+that first jump's target, `HIGH(RSTENT)`. Equality sends execution to
+`COLD`. With the overlay off, page zero normally holds `JP BF03h` instead.
+Its high byte is `BFh`, which sends `RSTENT` to warm boot
+(`src/boot.asm:21-47`).
+
+That second route handles an exit which reaches `0000h` with EXPAND 1
+still selected. The jump to `BF03h` then enters the unused part of that
+bank. Cold boot cleared it to zero so the CPU executes `NOP`s until it
+reaches the jump at `C000h`. Warm boot restores EXPAND 0 and attempts the
+pending cache writes. This route starts only after execution reaches the
+page-zero jump. A return through a stack hidden by EXPAND 1 has the separate
+stack problem explained in [Chapter 14](14-state-and-memory.md#expand-1-and-the-stack-boundary)
+(`src/boot.asm:21-47`, `src/boot.asm:178-215`, `src/boot.asm:321-346`).
+
+`COLD` disables maskable interrupts, sets `SP` to `8000h` and writes
+`LATCLAS`, `01h`, to the latch. Bit 0 removes the overlay and bit 2 leaves
+EXPAND 0 selected. It then selects interrupt mode 1, whose interrupt entry
+is `0038h` (`src/boot.asm:49-58`, `src/layout.asm:32-45`). Maskable
+interrupts stay disabled unless a program or service enables them.
+Routines such as the BIOS's `CEI` restore an enabled state only if it was
+saved on entry (`src/bios.asm:445-478`). The default restart code written
+at `0038h` contains another `EI`. Writing that instruction does not execute
+it and a qualifying resident jump there is preserved instead
+(`src/boot.asm:454-509`).
+
+## Cold-boot order
+
+Cold boot must preserve the old selection before clearing its evidence.
+It must also prove the banked RAM before moving the stack there.
+The calls in `COLD` establish those dependencies in this order
+(`src/boot.asm:49-158`):
+
+1. `DI`, the fixed-RAM boot stack at `8000h`, `LATCLAS` and interrupt
+   mode 1 establish the initial execution state (`src/boot.asm:49-58`).
+2. `RSLCAP` checks the old RAM and saves a recognised drive selection
+   before any of its evidence is cleared (`src/boot.asm:59-60`,
+   `src/boot.asm:240-312`).
+3. The page-zero clear writes all 256 bytes and sets `LATCHV`, the latch
+   shadow, to `LATCLAS` (`src/boot.asm:62-70`).
+4. `RAMCHK` checks the bank window at `8000h–BFFFh` and the second RAM bank
+   behind it. It returns only after both checks pass (`src/boot.asm:71-72`,
+   `src/boot.asm:579-610`).
+5. The workspace clear runs from `WSBASE` to `WSEND`. `SP` then moves
+   to `STKTOP`, the BDOS stack. No program is using that stack yet
+   (`src/boot.asm:73-86`).
+6. `CONINIT` starts serial input/output, reads the Matrix switch and probes
+   the PICO9918. A responding PICO9918 with that switch on becomes the
+   40-column console. Otherwise serial remains selected. `RTCHAS` probes
+   the clock and records its presence.
+   The banner goes to the selected console (`src/boot.asm:88-99`,
+   `src/console.asm:60-88`, `src/video.asm:22-29`, `src/pico.asm:69-90`).
+7. `MEMINIT` clears EXPAND 1 and marks every cache line empty before
+   mounting needs a cache line (`src/boot.asm:100-102`,
+   `src/boot.asm:321-346`).
+8. `COLDRT` sets the BDOS stack afresh. `LCDINIT` clears the 20 × 4 LCD
+   and the top row receives `TEC-OS 0.1  TEC-1G`
+   (`src/boot.asm:119-126`, `src/text.asm:67`).
+9. `SDINIT` starts the card. `MOUNT` reads and validates the container
+   table through the cache and fills the drive table. Either failure jumps
+   to `DIAG`, the diagnostic prompt (`src/boot.asm:127-132`,
+   `src/vol.asm:44-68`).
+10. `ALVINIT` reserves allocation vectors and the three-byte BDOS entry
+    stub. `RESEL` applies a saved selection only if it can match the
+    retained volumes to this card and fit their vector slots
+    (`src/boot.asm:133-137`, `src/boot.asm:695-800`, `src/volsvc.asm:31-159`).
+11. `PZTOP` receives the stub address as the top of the transient program area
+    (TPA). There are no loaded services yet (`src/boot.asm:139-143`).
+12. The ROM's seventeen BIOS jumps are copied to `BF00h`. Warm boot
+    leaves this RAM table in place, including replacement entries
+    (`src/boot.asm:144-152`, `src/layout.asm:134-135`).
+13. `SHOWVOL` prints one console line per mounted drive. `LCDVOLS` writes
+    the letters and labels of A and B to LCD rows 1 and 2
+    (`src/boot.asm:153-156`, `src/boot.asm:640-677`, `src/lcd.asm:122-155`).
+14. Page zero's drive/user byte receives A and user 0. Execution continues
+    into warm boot, whose `PZINIT` builds page zero once
+    (`src/boot.asm:157-199`).
+
+Clearing page zero also sets its IOBYTE to zero, the serial `TTY:` choice.
+`PISTART` changes the console field to 1, `CRT:`, only when the PICO9918
+responds and the Matrix switch enables the keyboard. It does so before the banner's
+first console call. With the Matrix switch off the console stays serial
+and `PISTART` leaves its console uninitialised even if the PICO9918 answers
+(`src/boot.asm:62-70`,
+`src/console.asm:82-88`, `src/video.asm:22-29`, `src/pico.asm:59-90`).
+
+The status LCD has bounded waits so its absence cannot stop boot.
+`LCDWAIT` polls the busy flag at most 256 times before each command or
+character. A missing display adds that bounded wait. Drives C and D have
+no LCD row. Row 3 carries `CARD?` at the diagnostic prompt or `GLCD?`
+after a failed `CON UC1` load of `GLCD.SVC` (`src/lcd.asm:29-58`,
+`src/lcd.asm:72-79`, `src/lcd.asm:122-155`, `src/boot.asm:817-829`,
+`src/loader.asm:885-913`). [Appendix G](appendix-g-tec-1g.md)
+gives the display's hardware connections.
+
+A diagnostic retry starts at step 8. `R` jumps to `COLDRT`, which resets
+the stack, clears the LCD and starts and mounts the card again. It keeps
+the existing memory and prints no second banner. `MOUNT` resets its table
+block marker so each required table block is read from the card afresh,
+even if a cache line already holds it (`src/boot.asm:119-132`,
+`src/boot.asm:831-846`, `src/vol.asm:56-65`, `src/vol.asm:463-529`).
+
+## RAM before the workspace
+
+The workspace and resident stacks need RAM at `8000h–BFFFh`. The cache
+and history need EXPAND 1 behind the same window. `RAMCHK` checks both
+before console initialisation, using the boot stack below `8000h`
+(`src/boot.asm:561-590`, `src/layout.asm:122`).
+
+`RAMPAT` first writes `55h` at `8000h` and `AAh` at `BFFFh`, then reads
+both back. It repeats with the patterns swapped. Writing both ends before
+reading either prevents a floating bus on a board with an empty U9 socket
+from returning the byte just written (`src/boot.asm:580-584`,
+`src/boot.asm:594-610`).
+
+The second check calls `DETECT` with `LATEXP` and address `8000h`.
+The routine saves the byte visible with the bit clear, selects the other
+view and saves that byte too. It writes the complement of the first byte
+with the bit set, then clears the bit and reads the address again
+(`src/boot.asm:358-384`, `src/boot.asm:585-589`).
+
+A changed byte in the original view means both selections reached the same
+RAM. An unchanged byte means the write reached a different region.
+`DETECT` puts both bytes back for two regions or only the original byte
+for one. It returns 1 or 2. No stack access occurs while the latch bit is
+set because that bit could hide the stack itself
+(`src/boot.asm:385-401`).
+
+A wrong end-byte or a count other than 2 enters `RAMBAD`. It sends
+`TEC-OS needs 64K RAM` directly through `TXBYTE` and stops in a loop.
+`TXBYTE` needs the serial port, ROM code and the fixed-RAM stack, not the
+uninitialised console workspace (`src/boot.asm:579-632`, `src/text.asm:10`,
+`src/console.asm:445-492`).
+
+After the workspace and console are ready, `MEMINIT` selects EXPAND 1 and
+zeros all 16 KiB. It fills the cache's 120 bytes of block keys with `FFh`
+to mark all 30 lines empty. It restores EXPAND 0 before returning.
+The banked stack is hidden throughout that clear so the routine uses
+registers and `LDIR` until the normal view is back
+(`src/boot.asm:314-346`, `src/layout.asm:50-58`).
+
+## Retained drive selection
+
+Power-on and the reset button reset the CPU in the same way. RAM after
+power-on has arbitrary contents while RAM after a button press can still
+contain TEC-OS's state. `RSLCAP` checks that state to recover the volume
+assignments made with `MOUNT`. It reads page zero, `STUBAD`, the three
+bytes addressed by `STUBAD` and the drive table. It executes none of that
+old RAM (`src/boot.asm:217-238`, `src/boot.asm:240-312`).
+
+Recognition requires all of these conditions:
+
+- `0000h` contains `JP BF03h`, the RAM BIOS warm-boot entry.
+- `0005h` contains a `JP` opcode.
+- The word in `STUBAD` points to three bytes containing `JP BENTRY`,
+  where `BENTRY` is this ROM's BDOS entry.
+- The word at `0006h` is at least `8000h`. It equals `STUBAD` or names
+  a page boundary no higher than `STUBAD`'s page
+  (`src/boot.asm:240-294`).
+
+The floor test comes before the comparison with `STUBAD`. The page-boundary
+case permits services to lower the entry word. `RSLCAP` does not compare
+it with `PZTOP`, which may still describe the earlier top during a service's
+`INIT`. A reset used to recover from a hung `INIT` can therefore pass.
+Recognition depends on this build's `STUBAD` location and `BENTRY` address.
+A different layout need not pass (`src/boot.asm:217-238`,
+`src/boot.asm:260-294`).
+
+On success `RSLCAP` copies the 32-byte drive table to `RSLBUF` at
+`0100h`. That fixed-RAM copy survives the clears and remains available
+through diagnostic retries. On failure it sets the saved A-drive geometry
+to zero, the marker for no retained selection. Cold boot then uses the
+card's default assignments (`src/boot.asm:296-312`,
+`src/layout.asm:165-175`, `src/volsvc.asm:31-37`).
+
+Cold boot clears page zero early and rebuilds its jumps only after the
+mount, as it enters warm boot. A reset during that incomplete interval,
+including a reset at the diagnostic prompt, fails recognition. The RAM no
+longer contains the required page-zero jumps (`src/boot.asm:62-70`,
+`src/boot.asm:127-158`, `src/boot.asm:194-195`).
+
+`RESEL` runs after the new card mount and allocation-vector placement.
+The slots and TPA therefore come from the card's normal allocation policy,
+not from the saved selection. Its first phase changes no drive assignment.
+It walks the card descriptors and compares each with every saved drive.
+The comparison covers the entry's eight bytes: start, length, geometry
+and flags. Each descriptor goes to the first matching saved drive and
+must fit its vector slot (`src/volsvc.asm:31-124`,
+`src/volsvc.asm:165-184`, `src/volsvc.asm:295-315`).
+
+The card's defaults remain in place after a failed read, an undersized slot
+or any retained drive left unmatched. Only a complete match permits the
+second phase. That phase copies the saved entries and matched indexes into
+the drive table and builds each mounted drive's DPH. It performs no card
+read and has no failure exit. A previously empty drive stays empty even if
+the card assigns a volume to that letter (`src/volsvc.asm:47-159`).
+
