 ## `Write lost`
 
 `Write lost` applies to a volume written behind, whose writes wait in the block
 cache. At each warm boot TEC-OS writes every changed block in the cache to the
-card. A block whose write fails is dropped, so the card keeps its old copy. If
-any write failed, the message is printed once, on a line of its own, and the
-rest of a chained line is dropped (`src/cache.asm:182-215`,
-`src/cache.asm:388-397`, `src/text.asm:69`).
-
-The changes in the dropped blocks are lost, and the next warm boot prints
-nothing more. A directory block whose write fails, or whose data blocks fail
-to write, is dropped during the run: the BDOS reports `Bad Sector` at once,
-and the warm boot after the key prints nothing. The data blocks that failed
-stay in the cache, so a close tried again fails again until the card takes
-them. The warm boot after the key writes them again and gives `Write lost` if
-they still fail (`src/cache.asm:182-215`, `src/cache.asm:378-386`). The file
-that was being written needs writing again, once the card is put right and
-the board reset.
-
-Changes still in the cache when a card comes out are lost, even with the same
-card put back, since only cold boot starts a card (`src/boot.asm:67`), and the
-first warm boot after it gives `Write lost`. BDOS function 48 reports a
-failure to a program at once: it writes every changed block and returns `A` =
-`FFh` if any write failed (`src/cpm/bdos.asm:175-180`).
